---
description: Require actionable checklists in project Markdown plans
alwaysApply: true
---

# Planning documents

- When creating or substantially updating an implementation plan or project
  Markdown document, include actionable Markdown checkboxes (`- [ ]`).
- Use unchecked boxes for work that is not verified and checked boxes only for
  work completed with evidence.
- A checked box's own sentence must stay true. Do not check a claim that the
  following note negates.
- When a gate defers implementation, leave those task boxes unchecked and keep
  their original sentences. Record the deferral in the linked evidence file and
  a short note under the step. Do not rewrite the tasks into checked deferrals.
- An unrun conditional feature is **not authorized**, not **pass** or **fail**.
  Use **fail** only after the pre-registered pass rule was scored.
- Keep every item under a `## Learning objectives` section unchecked. Those
  lists are study topics, not implementation or verification checklists.
- For verification checkboxes, record or link the command and result when the
  evidence is not obvious from the changed files.
- Before running or recording a verification command, confirm every referenced
  file, test path, marker, and CLI option exists in the current checkout.
- When authoring an implementation plan, confirm every linked repo path in
  **Parent**, **Evidence**, contracts, and **Verification** exists. If a
  canonical path is missing, add an **Open baseline gaps** checkbox instead of
  a dead link; do not mark authoring complete until the gap is resolved or
  explicitly recorded.
- Use **`## Out of scope for this document`** (or **`## Out of scope`**) for
  excluded work in implementation plans. Do not use only **Non-goals** when the
  plan already has a dedicated exclusion section.
- Do not mark full-suite, integration, or lint checks complete when they were
  not run successfully; distinguish unavailable tooling from a clean result.
- Recheck broad verification claims after later milestones change the codebase,
  and uncheck or annotate them when newer evidence invalidates the claim.
- Keep the checklist close to the relevant steps so it can be updated as work
  progresses.
- Do not commit or push planning-document changes without explicit user
  approval.
- When closing a workstream milestone, sync the plan **Status**, **Execution
  sequence**, **Acceptance criteria**, and linked **evidence** file so unchecked
  boxes do not contradict implemented code or recorded deferrals.
- When close-out records regenerated `artifacts/`, verification file hashes, or
  code changes (e.g. remediation Steps 2–7), run `git status` on those canonical
  paths before setting **Status** to `closed`. If they are still modified or
  untracked, either commit them (user-approved `/commit`) or keep **Status**
  explicit (`closed (docs only)` or `in progress — commit bundle pending`) and
  list the paths in evidence; do not imply the remote or default branch already
  contains the verified package.
- When marking an implementation **step** complete in a workstream plan, update
  that step’s checkboxes and evidence in the same pass, and refresh **Current
  baseline** (especially **Open baseline gaps** and the canonical module table)
  so the plan does not still describe the pre-step codebase. Do not rely on a
  later review to discover drift.
- When a plan mixes **documentation authoring** with a later **user-gated** step
  (e.g. “OK to start Phase N code”), split **Goal / Exit** and **Verification**
  into two labeled gates. Mark authoring **closed** only after deliverable docs
  exist; leave explicit user-approval checkboxes unchecked until the user
  approves in writing. Do not use a single “Phase complete” verdict that blurs
  the two.
- On close, align **Status**, parent transition-doc section numbers (e.g. § 2.4
  exit criteria), and **Acceptance criteria** so section references and status
  strings (`draft`, `planning-complete`, `closed (authoring)`) match the linked
  child plan.
- When a feature card defines an **analysis protocol** with multiple samples
  (primary, contrast, or discussion-only), map **each** sample to an explicit
  workstream plan step and evidence subsection. Pass/fail criteria do not
  replace omitted protocol rows (e.g. Strategy C contrast aggregates when
  clustering Strategy B false positives only).
- Workstream **implementation plans** must link the promoted feature card and
  mirror its **pre-registered pass/fail** in gate stopping rules. Do not invent
  a decision path that contradicts the card (e.g. `manifest_fixable_gaps` with
  `repro_blocked_gaps = 0` still **pass** → `keep_manual`, not a forced
  `manifest_fix_only`). Cite the card section in Gate B.
- In the plan **Goal** block, include **Status**, **Evidence**, **Parent**, and
  **Card** lines when a dedicated evidence file exists (match closed workstream
  plans such as WS7).
- Step 1 **audit corpus** must list both the feature-card minimum inputs and any
  broader scope named in the parent implementation plan (e.g. all paper results
  in WS5–8), so execution matches the matrix scope.
- Markdown **field tables** in plans: one line per row; put long definitions in
  a short note below the table, not a wrapped cell (avoids broken tables).
- Multi-step workstream **evidence**: Step 1 “open gap” tables and pre-edit
  snapshots must be labeled **historical** or reconciled in the same pass when
  a later step closes them. The evidence file **Status** and final sections
  must not contradict earlier rows (e.g. “sync open” after Step 3 closed).
- After a **read-only audit** step, label evidence **code inspection** (and
  similar narrative) as a **historical snapshot** at that step’s close once a
  later step edits the same paths. State explicitly when on-disk `artifacts/`
  or `experiments/` JSON was not regenerated (inventory rows still describe
  frozen files).
- Close-out checkboxes that describe **forbidden later work** (e.g. “do not
  start Step 2”) must be phrased so they stay true after later steps run—use
  **at Step N close** wording (“At Step 1 close, Step 2 was not started”) rather
  than an absolute future claim checked `[x]`.
- Plan **Verification** blocks must cite commands that actually pass in this
  repo. If a check needs environment variables (e.g. `docker compose config` and
  `SSCRE_ADMIN_TOKEN`), document the same non-secret sample env as CI (see
  `.github/workflows/ci.yml`); do not list a bare command that fails by design
  and record it as pass elsewhere.
